\id LUK - Aperto Bible (English) v4.3
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Luke
\toc1 The Gospel According to Luke
\toc2 Luke
\toc3 Lk
\mt1 Luke

\c 3
\s1 A Voice in the Wilderness
\p
\v 1 \x + \xo 3:1-2 \xt See Sidebar: The Political Synchronism — Why Luke Names the Powers\x* The fifteenth year of the reign of Emperor Tiberius.\f + \fr 3:1 \fk TEXT \fq Tiberius \ft Tiberius Claudius Nero (r. AD 14–37). In many provinces of the empire he was worshipped as a divine figure — the emperor the Roman world called a god. Luke names him first before pivoting to the God who actually speaks.\f*\f + \fr 3:1 \fk APOLOGETICS \fq fifteenth year \ft Luke's synchronism — six officials named, seven time markers — is ancient historiographical practice for anchoring events precisely. Pontius Pilate as governor is confirmed by the Pilate Stone discovered at Caesarea Maritima (1961). Lysanias as tetrarch of Abilene, once thought anachronistic, is now attested by a Greek inscription from the region. The fifteen-year marker places John's ministry in 28/29 CE — consistent with Josephus (Antiquities 18.5.2) and archaeological evidence. Luke is doing journalism, not mythology.\f* Pontius Pilate governing Judea. Herod tetrarch of Galilee —\add a subordinate king,\add* ruling at Rome's pleasure. His brother Philip ruling Ituraea and Trachonitis. Lysanias tetrarch of Abilene.
\v 2 High priests Annas and Caiaphas.\f + \fr 3:2 \fk KULTUR \fq Annas and Caiaphas \ft Jewish law prescribed one high priest. Annas served AD 6–15 but was deposed by Rome; his son-in-law Caiaphas held the official title (AD 18–36). Both wielded real power — a portrait of compromised religious authority under occupation.\f* \add Seven names. Seven layers of empire and occupation and compromised religion.\add*
\p
And then: the word of God came\x + \xo 3:2 \xt See Sidebar: Wilderness Theology — The Desert as Sacred Space\x*\f + \fr 3:2 \fk TEXT \fq word of God came (ῥῆμα θεοῦ) \ft The exact LXX prophetic commissioning formula — used at the opening of Jeremiah (1:1), Ezekiel (1:3), Hosea, Joel, and others. After 400 years of prophetic silence, God speaks again. Not in Rome. Not in Jerusalem. In the wilderness.\f* to John son of Zechariah. In the wilderness\add — that emptied, listening place\add*.
\p
\add After generations of silence, God spoke again.\add*
\p
\v 3 John traveled through the entire region around the Jordan, preaching a baptism of repentance\f + \fr 3:3 \fk TEXT \fq repentance (μετάνοια) \ft Not guilt, but orientation — a complete turning of direction. John wasn't preaching emotional remorse. He was calling people to change the direction of their lives, which in first-century Palestine carried concrete social and economic implications.\f*\f + \fr 3:3 \fk LEBEN \fq repentance \ft Christian traditions have understood this turning differently, and each offers something. For Catholics and Orthodox, repentance is sacramental — externalized in confession, absolution, acts of restitution. For Protestants, it is interior — a change of mind that produces changed behavior. For Anabaptist and liberation traditions, repentance is structural — it requires changing one's relationship to power, money, and the marginalized. John's audience in this passage includes crowds (lifestyle), tax collectors (money), and soldiers (power). The text seems to say: all three.\f* —\add a turning —\add* for the forgiveness of sins. \v 4 As Isaiah\f + \fr 3:4-6 \fk KONTEXT \fq Isaiah \ft Luke quotes Isaiah 40:3–5 more fully than Matthew or Mark, including the universal climax: “all flesh shall see God's salvation” (v.6). This is Luke's theological thesis. The chapter opens naming seven rulers in a specific corner of empire; it closes with every human being everywhere.\f* had written:
\q1 A voice cries out in the wilderness:
\q2 Prepare the way of the Lord,
\q2 make his paths straight.
\v 5 \q1 Every valley shall be filled in,
\q2 every mountain and hill brought low.
\q1 The crooked will become straight,
\q2 the rough ways smooth.
\v 6 \q1 And all flesh shall see God's salvation.

\s1 Brood of Vipers
\p
\v 7 \x + \xo 3:3-16 \xt See Sidebar: John's Baptism — How It Differed from Temple Practices\x* Crowds streamed out to the Jordan to be baptized.\add John didn't ease them in.\add*
\p
“You brood of vipers!\f + \fr 3:7 \fk TEXT \fq brood of vipers (γεννήματα ἐχιδνῶν) \ft Literally “offspring of vipers.” Not merely an insult — an accusation of origin. Children of the ancient serpent (Genesis 3), children of deception rather than of Abraham. Standard prophetic invective, designed to shatter complacency before it offers hope.\f* \add The words landed before they'd gathered themselves.\add* Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?
\v 8 Bear fruit that shows you mean it. And don't start thinking, 'We have Abraham as our father'\f + \fr 3:8 \fk KULTUR \fq Abraham as our father \ft Many in first-century Judaism believed Abrahamic lineage provided a kind of divine safety net — ethnic privilege as spiritual insurance. John's counter — “God can raise up children for Abraham from these stones” — demolishes that assumption. The covenant continues through transformed lives, not inherited bloodlines.\f* — God can raise up children for Abraham from these stones.
\v 9 The axe is already at the root of the trees. Every tree without good fruit gets cut down and thrown in the fire.”
\p
\v 10 “What should we do?” the crowds asked.
\p
\v 11 “If you have two coats, give one to someone who has none. If you have food, do the same.”\f + \fr 3:11 \fk LEBEN \fq two coats \ft This is the simplest ethic in the New Testament — and one of the hardest. John doesn't call for revolution or legislation. He points to the specific surplus in hand: two coats when someone nearby has none. Christian traditions across centuries have applied this differently — Catholic social teaching (solidarity and the preferential option for the poor), Quaker simplicity (live at the level of genuine need, not comfort), Protestant stewardship (hold material goods loosely). The question the text raises in any era: what is the equivalent of my second coat?\f*
\p
\v 12 Tax collectors came too.\add These were Jews who collected revenue for Rome, presumed cheats, shut out of decent religious company. That they were here at all said something.\add*\f + \fr 3:12 \fk KULTUR \fq tax collectors (τελῶναι) \ft Local toll collectors — Jews who subcontracted Roman revenue collection, despised as collaborators and exploiters of their own people. In honor-shame culture (Bruce Malina), they were social and religious outcasts. Their presence among those seeking baptism was genuinely surprising.\f* “Teacher, what should we do?”
\p
\v 13 “Collect only what's required.\add Not a penny more.\add*”
\p
\v 14 Soldiers also asked —\add Herod's troops, armed and accustomed to taking what they wanted.\add* “And us — what should we do?”
\p
“Don't extort anyone. Don't bring false charges. Be content with your wages.”\f + \fr 3:14 \fk LEBEN \fq Be content with your wages \ft John's answer to those with coercive power is not “give it up” but “don't abuse it.” Three prohibitions — extortion, false accusation, general shakedown — describe the specific temptations of institutional power in any era. The application transfers readily: authority in any form (management, law enforcement, bureaucracy, medicine, parenting) faces the same temptations: leveraging power for personal gain, misusing information to harm, bending rules for preference. John doesn't moralize. He names the specific distortions and demands they stop.\f*

\s1 One Mightier Than I
\p
\v 15 The people had begun to wonder.\add Something was moving through the crowd — a question held carefully, the kind of hope that doesn't say its name out loud.\add* Could John be the Messiah?\f + \fr 3:15 \fk TEXT \fq Messiah (χριστός) \ft “The Anointed One” — the king God had promised, who would liberate Israel and bring justice. Under Roman occupation, longing for the Messiah was especially intense, and John was a figure who matched no previous category.\f*\f + \fr 3:15 \fk LEBEN \fq hope that doesn't say its name out loud \ft The crowd's unspoken question — “is this the one?” — is more universal than its Jewish context suggests. Longing for justice to come, for things to be set right, for someone to break through — this is human in any era. Political disappointment, institutional failure, personal suffering all feed the same silent question. What the text adds: even genuine longing can misidentify its object. The crowd is right to hope; they have the wrong candidate. Hope must learn discernment, not merely passion.\f*
\p
\v 16 John answered them all: “I baptize you with water. But someone mightier than I is coming. I am not worthy to untie the strap of his sandals\f + \fr 3:16 \fk KULTUR \fq sandal strap \ft Removing sandals was the work of non-Jewish household slaves — even disciples wouldn't do it for their teacher (Craig Keener). John's self-placement below this threshold is extreme: he isn't even the lowest servant in the household of the Coming One.\f* —\add a task reserved for non-Jewish slaves, beneath even what a disciple would do for his teacher.\add* He will baptize you with Holy Spirit and fire.\f + \fr 3:16 \fk KONTEXT \fq Holy Spirit and fire \ft Different traditions emphasize different aspects: Pentecostal — empowering fire (Acts 2:3). Catholic and Orthodox — purifying fire of sanctification. Protestant — judging fire of divine wrath on sin. Luke likely intends purification and judgment together, with Pentecost as partial fulfillment and final judgment as completion.\f*
\v 17 His winnowing fork is\add already\add* in his hand.\add Think of the threshing floor: grain tossed into the air, the wind taking the chaff, the wheat falling back. That separation, final and complete.\add* He will gather the wheat into his barn. And the chaff he will burn with unquenchable fire.”
\p
\v 18 With many other words, John proclaimed good news\f + \fr 3:18 \fk TEXT \fq good news (εὐηγγελίζετο) \ft Luke calls John's harsh preaching “gospel” — the same word used for Jesus' ministry. Warning and hope are inseparable: even talk of axes and unquenchable fire serves the goal of rescue. God speaks in judgment because God desires to save.\f* to the people.

\s1 Herod Silences the Prophet
\p
\v 19 But\add Herod Antipas,\add* the tetrarch, had been\add publicly\add* rebuked by John. For Herodias, his brother's wife\f + \fr 3:19 \fk KULTUR \fq Herodias \ft This marriage violated Torah on multiple counts: Herodias had divorced her first husband Philip (a woman initiating divorce was nearly unprecedented), married his half-brother while Philip still lived (adultery), and entered a union the law classified as incestuous (Leviticus 18:16). John's rebuke was not moralizing — it was legal indictment of a reigning ruler.\f* —\add a marriage that violated the law in more than one direction.\add* And for every other evil thing he'd done.
\v 20 So Herod added\add one more to the list of his evils:\add* he locked John in prison.\f + \fr 3:20 \fk LEBEN \fq locked John in prison \ft Luke records John's imprisonment without drama or explanation — just a sentence, then silence. The Greek word for witness (μάρτυς) eventually became “martyr” because those who spoke truth to power so often paid for it. Church history across every tradition is populated with figures who named what shouldn't be named and were silenced — Bonhoeffer, Oscar Romero, the martyrs of early Christianity and modern persecution alike. The text doesn't say faithfulness guarantees safety. It says faithfulness is worth it anyway.\f*
\p
\add The voice was silenced.\add*

\s1 Jesus Is Baptized
\p
\v 21 When all the people were being baptized, Jesus was baptized too.\add Not before them. Not apart from them. He stood in line with everyone else.\add*
\p
Then he prayed.\f + \fr 3:21 \fk TEXT \fq while he was praying (προσευχομένου) \ft Only Luke notes that Jesus was praying at his baptism. This is characteristic of Luke's Gospel: Jesus prays before choosing the Twelve (6:12), before Peter's confession (9:18), at the Transfiguration (9:28–29), in Gethsemane (22:41–44). In Luke's telling, heaven opens and God acts in the context of prayer.\f*
\p
Heaven opened.\f + \fr 3:21 \fk KONTEXT \fq heaven opened \ft An apocalyptic signal from Jewish literature (Ezekiel 1:1). Isaiah 64:1 had pleaded: “Oh, that you would tear open the heavens and come down!” Luke presents Jesus' baptism as God's answer to that ancient prayer.\f*
\p
\v 22 The Holy Spirit descended on him in bodily form, like a dove\add settling\add*.\f + \fr 3:22 \fk TEXT \fq in bodily form (σωματικῷ εἴδει) \ft Luke's unique phrase. Matthew and Mark simply say “like a dove.” Luke insists: this was visible, physical — not merely inner experience or symbolic vision. Something happened that could be seen.\f* \add In bodily form — Luke alone says this, and the phrase is deliberate. Something happened that could be seen.\add*
\p
A voice came from heaven: “You are my beloved Son.\f + \fr 3:22 \fk TEXT \fq beloved Son (ὁ υἱός μου ὁ ἀγαπητός) \ft The heavenly voice simultaneously echoes three Old Testament passages: Psalm 2:7 (royal Messiah — “You are my Son”), Genesis 22:2 (the beloved Isaac, offered up), and Isaiah 42:1 (the suffering Servant). Jesus is at once King, sacrifice, and servant. His identity and his vocation arrive together in this single word: beloved.\f*\f + \fr 3:22 \fk LEBEN \fq You are my beloved Son \ft This voice comes before Jesus has done anything. No miracles yet. No sermon. No disciples. Before any achievement, a declaration: beloved. The sequence matters: identity first, then vocation. Christian traditions have drawn different things from this — the mystical tradition (Meister Eckhart, Thomas Merton) understands it as a disclosure of what is already true of humanity restored in Christ; the contemplative tradition reads it as a pattern for spiritual life — receiving before striving; the Reformation tradition emphasizes that this declaration comes by divine gift, not human merit. All agree on the direction: from God to person, not the reverse.\f* In you I am well pleased.”\f + \fr 3:22 \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq voice from heaven \ft Modern readers often struggle with divine voices. The text assumes God communicates directly with humans — not as metaphor but as historical event. The range of interpretations: inner spiritual experience made vivid through narrative; literary device to convey theological truth about Jesus' identity; objective divine speech (the Jewish bat qol was an accepted mode of post-prophetic divine communication). The text presents it as witnessed event. How one receives this depends on prior assumptions about whether God acts in history.\f*\f + \fr 3:22 \fk APOLOGETICS \fq Jesus was baptized \ft The baptism of Jesus by John is considered by historians one of the most historically reliable events in the Gospel tradition, precisely because it was theologically awkward: why would the sinless Jesus undergo a baptism of repentance? This criterion of embarrassment — early Christians would have had every reason to minimize or omit it, yet all four Gospels report it — is a standard test for historical reliability (John Meier, N.T. Wright, James Dunn). Josephus independently confirms John as a historical figure (Antiquities 18.5.2). The early church struggled to explain the baptism (Matthew adds John's protest, 3:14-15), which is precisely why historians trust its historicity.\f*

\s1 Jesus's Family Tree
\p
\v 23 Jesus was about thirty years old\f + \fr 3:23 \fk KULTUR \fq thirty years old \ft Thirty was the traditional age of entry into public service: David became king at thirty (2 Samuel 5:4), Joseph entered Pharaoh's service at thirty (Genesis 41:46), Levites began temple ministry at thirty (Numbers 4:3). Jesus begins at the properly mature age.\f* when he began. He was known as the son of Joseph.\f + \fr 3:23 \fk TEXT \fq known as (ὡς ἐνομίζετο) \ft The Greek verb means “was legally reckoned” — not “everyone guessed” but “was legally recognized as.” Joseph was Jesus' legal father. Luke quietly holds the tension between legal paternity and divine conception without resolving it here.\f*
\p
\add Before moving forward, Luke looks backward. He traces the line — not from Abraham forward to an heir, but in reverse: through kings, through patriarchs, past the flood, all the way to the first human being. A genealogy that makes a claim: Jesus belongs not only to Israel's story, but to every human story ever told.\add*
\p
Joseph was son of Heli,
\v 24 son of Matthat, son of Levi, son of Melchi, son of Jannai, son of Joseph,
\v 25 son of Mattathias, son of Amos, son of Nahum, son of Esli, son of Naggai,
\v 26 son of Maath, son of Mattathias, son of Semein, son of Josech, son of Joda,
\v 27 son of Joanan, son of Rhesa, son of Zerubbabel,\f + \fr 3:27 \fk TEXT \fq Zerubbabel \ft Led the return from Babylonian exile (Ezra 3:2). A Davidic heir, seen by prophets as a potential messianic figure (Haggai 1:1; Zechariah 4). His inclusion links Jesus to Israel's post-exilic restoration hope.\f* son of Shealtiel, son of Neri,
\v 28 son of Melchi, son of Addi, son of Cosam, son of Elmadam, son of Er,
\v 29 son of Joshua, son of Eliezer, son of Jorim, son of Matthat, son of Levi,
\v 30 son of Simeon, son of Judah, son of Joseph, son of Jonam, son of Eliakim,
\v 31 son of Melea, son of Menna, son of Mattatha, son of Nathan,\f + \fr 3:31 \fk KONTEXT \fq Nathan \ft Luke traces the Davidic line through Nathan, David's son, while Matthew follows the royal line through Solomon. Different ancient traditions preserved different genealogical records. Both Gospel writers affirm Jesus' Davidic descent — essential for messianic identity — arriving there through different routes.\f* son of David,
\v 32 son of Jesse, son of Obed, son of Boaz, son of Salmon, son of Nahshon,
\v 33 son of Amminadab, son of Admin, son of Arni, son of Hezron, son of Perez, son of Judah,
\v 34 son of Jacob, son of Isaac, son of Abraham,\f + \fr 3:34 \fk KONTEXT \fq Abraham \ft Matthew's genealogy ends here — Jesus as fulfillment of Jewish promises. Luke continues past Abraham to Adam, universalizing the scope. Matthew presents Jesus the Jewish Messiah; Luke presents Jesus the Savior of all humanity. Both emphases are essential.\f* son of Terah, son of Nahor,
\v 35 son of Serug, son of Reu, son of Peleg, son of Eber, son of Shelah,
\v 36 son of Cainan, son of Arphaxad, son of Shem, son of Noah,\f + \fr 3:36 \fk TEXT \fq Noah \ft The line reaches past the flood — back before nations were divided, before the judgment of Babel. Noah was the ancestor of all surviving humanity.\f* son of Lamech,
\v 37 son of Methuselah, son of Enoch, son of Jared, son of Mahalaleel, son of Cainan,
\v 38 son of Enos, son of Seth, son of Adam,\f + \fr 3:38 \fk KONTEXT \fq Adam \ft The genealogy culminates in Adam — not Abraham, not Noah, but the first human being. Luke's Jesus belongs to the entire human family. What was broken at the beginning (Genesis 3) finds in Jesus the one through whom restoration comes.\f* son of God.\f + \fr 3:38 \fk TEXT \fq son of God (τοῦ θεοῦ) \ft Adam was created directly by God (Genesis 2:7) and is thus “son of God” by creation. Luke creates an inclusion: the heavenly voice just declared Jesus “my beloved Son” (v.22), and now the genealogy traces back to Adam “son of God.” Both are sons of God — but Jesus is Son in the deepest and final sense.\f*\f + \fr 3:38 \fk LEBEN \fq son of Adam, son of God \ft The genealogy ends here — not with a title, not with a doxology, just two short phrases: son of Adam, son of God. The theological weight is available to anyone willing to stand in it: every human being is descended from the one Luke just connected to God. Human dignity in this reading is not earned, not conferred by merit or achievement or belonging to the right group. It is structural. The Imago Dei — the image of God — belongs to the species. This is why Christian traditions across every era have found in this text a basis for care for the poor, for human rights, for opposition to slavery and exploitation: not sentiment, but genealogy.\f*
